      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + x^{3} \\cdot y = - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n- **II.** $y' - y \\cdot \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} = 4 \\cdot y^{2} \\cdot \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y' + 3 \\cdot y = \\frac{5 \\cdot \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}}{y^{2}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x^{3},\\quad q(x)=-1,\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=4 \\cdot \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad n=2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3,\\quad q(x)=5 \\cdot \\sec{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad n=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select I, II, III.",
